; ======================================================================
;  thermal.pi4 - how hot the BCM2711 is, from the part and from the
;  firmware, with the part preferred and the firmware as the fallback.
;
;  TWO SOURCES, AND THEY ARE NOT INTERCHANGEABLE
;
;    THE REGISTER. The SoC carries an AVS ring-oscillator temperature
;    monitor and one of its registers is a plain read: no mailbox, no
;    buffer, no cache maintenance, nothing that can be starved by a busy
;    firmware. It answers in about the time a load takes, which is what
;    makes a fan policy that runs on every tick affordable.
;
;    THE MAILBOX. GET_TEMPERATURE, which this tree has been asking the
;    firmware since the `info` command existed. It costs a property-
;    buffer round trip, it can be starved (see mailbox.pi4's notes on
;    the framebuffer and the MBOX1 wedge), and it is the ONLY source for
;    the throttle temperature - the register block has no "and this is
;    where I start slowing down" field.
;
;  SO: temperature comes from the register when the register says its
;  reading is valid, and from the mailbox when it does not. The maximum
;  comes from the mailbox always. Which one answered is recorded and
;  reported, because a board that has quietly fallen back to the mailbox
;  is a board whose fan policy now costs a round trip per tick, and that
;  should be visible rather than deduced.
;
;  EVERY NUMBER IN THIS FILE IS CELSIUS, AND NOTHING IN THIS FILE IS
;  EVER PRINTED. Project ruling, 2026-09-07: every temperature a person
;  reads is Fahrenheit. This library is the half of that rule that keeps
;  its Celsius, and deliberately: the AVS coefficients come out of a
;  device tree in millidegrees Celsius and the firmware answers
;  GET_TEMPERATURE in millidegrees Celsius, so a file rewritten into
;  Fahrenheit could no longer be read line by line against the documents
;  quoted below - which is the only reason to trust it. Convert at the
;  edge, not in the maths.
;
;  THE CONVERSION IS Anvil/Hal/hal.pbi's HwTempMilliF()/HwTempWholeF(),
;  once, for the whole tree. It is there rather than here because it is
;  pure arithmetic rather than a fact about a BCM2711, and because the
;  UNO Q has a fan command and no thermal library at all - a copy here
;  would have to be matched by a copy there, and two copies of one
;  conversion is how two lines of the same report come to differ by a
;  degree.
;
;  REQUIRES, in this order, in the main file (house rule 10):
;      XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/mailbox.pi4"    ; MailboxTemperature
;      XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/thermal.pi4"
;
;  HARDWARE-VERIFIED (2026-09-06, pi4-fan-silicon). The REGISTER half
;  has now been read on the part: `fan` on the board printed 39 C
;  (39920 millidegrees) sourced from "a sensor register the board reads
;  directly", i.e. AVS_RO_TEMP_STATUS at $FD5D2000+$200 with both
;  validity bits set, alongside the firmware's 85 C throttle point from
;  the mailbox. The address, the two validity bits and the negative
;  slope are therefore measured and not only cited.
;
; ======================================================================
;  THE REGISTER, AND WHERE ITS NUMBERS COME FROM
; ======================================================================
;  THE BLOCK. Datasheets/pi4/bcm2711.dtsi:68-77
;
;      avs_monitor: avs-monitor@7d5d2000 {
;          compatible = "brcm,bcm2711-avs-monitor", "syscon", "simple-mfd";
;          reg = <0x7d5d2000 0xf00>;
;          thermal: thermal { compatible = "brcm,bcm2711-thermal"; ... };
;      };
;
;  THE ADDRESS TRANSLATION IS THE DEVICE TREE'S OWN, and it is not the
;  familiar one. bcm2711.dtsi:40-42 gives the soc node three ranges:
;
;      <0x7e000000  0x0 0xfe000000  0x01800000>   the common peripherals
;      <0x7c000000  0x0 0xfc000000  0x02000000>   the BCM2711-only ones
;      <0x40000000  0x0 0xff800000  0x00800000>   the ARM-local block
;
;  0x7d5d2000 falls in the SECOND range, not the first, so it is
;  $FD5D2000 from the processor and not $FE5D2000. Getting that wrong
;  reads a different peripheral and the validity bits below are what
;  would catch it.
;
;  THE REGISTER, ITS VALIDITY BITS AND ITS FIELD.
;  Datasheets/pi4/pwm/rpi-6.12.y_bcm2711_thermal.c:24-26
;
;      #define AVS_RO_TEMP_STATUS            0x200
;      #define AVS_RO_TEMP_STATUS_VALID_MSK  (BIT(16) | BIT(10))
;      #define AVS_RO_TEMP_STATUS_DATA_MSK   GENMASK(9, 0)
;
;  and its read (same file, lines 42-51) is exactly: read, refuse with
;  -EIO if EITHER validity bit is clear, mask to ten bits, convert.
;
;  THE CONVERSION IS A STRAIGHT LINE and its two coefficients are in the
;  device tree, not the driver. bcm2711.dtsi:626-629
;
;      &cpu_thermal { coefficients = <(-487) 410040>; ... };
;
;  which the driver reads as slope and offset and applies as
;
;      *temp = slope * val + offset;             (bcm2711_thermal.c:51)
;
;  in MILLIDEGREES - the comment one line above says so: "Convert a HW
;  code to a temperature reading (millidegree celsius)".
;
;  THE SLOPE IS NEGATIVE, which is the one thing about this sensor that
;  will trip a reader: a BIGGER code is a COLDER part. Code 0 would be
;  410 C and code 1023 would be -88 C, so the whole useful band sits
;  near the top of the range - about 739 at 50 C and 698 at 70 C. A
;  conversion written with the sign the other way round produces
;  plausible-looking numbers that move the wrong way, which is why the
;  gate checks the DIRECTION as well as the values.
; ======================================================================

#AVS_MONITOR_BASE     = $FD5D2000
#AVS_RO_TEMP_STATUS   = $200
#AVS_TEMP_VALID_MASK  = $10400        ; BIT(16) | BIT(10)
#AVS_TEMP_DATA_MASK   = $3FF          ; bits 9:0

; The two coefficients, named separately so a reader can check each one
; against the device tree line rather than against a folded constant.
#AVS_TEMP_SLOPE  = -487
#AVS_TEMP_OFFSET = 410040

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  SOURCES, and #THERM_NONE.
;
;  #THERM_NONE is not zero and not -1, and both of those matter. Zero is
;  a real temperature (this part in a freezer) and -1 is a real
;  temperature (colder still); a sentinel that collides with a reading
;  is how a fan ends up off in a hot room. -273151 is a millidegree
;  value below absolute zero, so it can never be a measurement, and it
;  is far enough from every real reading that a caller comparing with
;  the wrong operator gets an obviously absurd number rather than a
;  nearly-right one.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
#THERM_NONE = -273151

#THERM_SRC_NONE    = 0        ; nothing answered
#THERM_SRC_REGISTER = 1       ; the AVS monitor
#THERM_SRC_MAILBOX  = 2       ; the firmware property interface

Global therm_source.i = #THERM_SRC_NONE
Global therm_regFails.i = 0   ; how many times the register was invalid
Global therm_lastRaw.i = 0    ; the last raw status word, for the bench

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  ThermalRegRaw() - the status word, unmasked, as an unsigned 32-bit
;  value.
;
;  READING THIS ADDRESS CANNOT HANG THIS BOARD. That is the Pi 4's
;  measured property and it is written down in
;  RaspberryPi4/Board/hw_addr.pi4: no address on this part is known to
;  stall the interconnect, and an address with nothing behind it returns
;  a value rather than wedging - unlike the Arduino Q's QCM2290, where
;  exactly this kind of speculative read cost a power cycle. So the
;  register may be read before anything has proved the AVS block is
;  clocked, and the validity bits are what turn a garbage answer into a
;  fallback instead of into a temperature.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i ThermalRegRaw()
  Define v.i
  a64_barrier()
  v = PeekN(#AVS_MONITOR_BASE + #AVS_RO_TEMP_STATUS) & $FFFFFFFF
  a64_barrier()
  therm_lastRaw = v
  ProcedureReturn v
EndProcedure

; ThermalRegValid(raw) - 1 when BOTH validity bits are set. The driver
; tests the pair together and so does this: bit 16 alone, or bit 10
; alone, is not a reading the vendor's own code would use.
Procedure.i ThermalRegValid(raw.i)
  If (raw & #AVS_TEMP_VALID_MASK) = #AVS_TEMP_VALID_MASK
    ProcedureReturn 1
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn 0
EndProcedure

; ThermalCodeToMilliC(code) - the line, and nothing else. Separated from
; the read so the gate can drive it over the whole 0..1023 domain
; without a register model in the way.
Procedure.i ThermalCodeToMilliC(code.i)
  ProcedureReturn #AVS_TEMP_SLOPE * (code & #AVS_TEMP_DATA_MASK) + #AVS_TEMP_OFFSET
EndProcedure

; ThermalRegMilliC() - the register path's answer, or #THERM_NONE.
Procedure.i ThermalRegMilliC()
  Define raw.i
  raw = ThermalRegRaw()
  If ThermalRegValid(raw) = 0
    therm_regFails = therm_regFails + 1
    ProcedureReturn #THERM_NONE
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn ThermalCodeToMilliC(raw)
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  ThermalMailboxMilliC() - the firmware path.
;
;  MailboxTemperature() returns the firmware's millidegrees and 0 when
;  the call failed - which is mailbox.pi4's convention and is fine there
;  because 0 C from this tag has never been seen and the caller can ask
;  MailboxError() why. It is NOT fine here, because this file's
;  no-answer value has to be one that cannot collide with a reading, so
;  a 0 from the mailbox is translated into #THERM_NONE.
;
;  THAT TRANSLATION LOSES A REAL 0 C READING and that is the right trade
;  in this direction: a Pi 4 at exactly 0.000 C would be reported as "no
;  answer" and the fan would fall back to the register or to its own
;  safe default, which is correct behaviour for a freezing board. The
;  opposite trade - treating a failed call as 0 C - is a fan that never
;  starts.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i ThermalMailboxMilliC()
  Define t.i
  t = MailboxTemperature()
  If t = 0
    ProcedureReturn #THERM_NONE
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn t
EndProcedure

; ThermalMaxMilliC() - the temperature at which the firmware starts
; throttling. MAILBOX ONLY: the AVS block reports what it measures and
; has no field for what the firmware intends to do about it, so there is
; no register path to prefer here and none is pretended.
Procedure.i ThermalMaxMilliC()
  Define t.i
  t = MailboxTemperatureMax()
  If t = 0
    ProcedureReturn #THERM_NONE
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn t
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  ThermalMilliC() - THE ONE A CALLER WANTS. Register first, mailbox
;  second, #THERM_NONE if neither answered, and ThermalSource() says
;  which it was.
;
;  THE SOURCE IS SET ON EVERY CALL, including the failing one. A stale
;  source left over from the last successful read would report REGISTER
;  while the mailbox is quietly carrying the load.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i ThermalMilliC()
  Define t.i
  t = ThermalRegMilliC()
  If t <> #THERM_NONE
    therm_source = #THERM_SRC_REGISTER
    ProcedureReturn t
  EndIf
  t = ThermalMailboxMilliC()
  If t <> #THERM_NONE
    therm_source = #THERM_SRC_MAILBOX
    ProcedureReturn t
  EndIf
  therm_source = #THERM_SRC_NONE
  ProcedureReturn #THERM_NONE
EndProcedure

Procedure.i ThermalSource()
  ProcedureReturn therm_source
EndProcedure

Procedure.i ThermalRegisterFailures()
  ProcedureReturn therm_regFails
EndProcedure

Procedure.i ThermalLastRaw()
  ProcedureReturn therm_lastRaw
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  ThermalWholeC(milli) - millidegrees to whole degrees, rounded to
;  NEAREST and correct on both sides of zero.
;
;  Integer division truncates toward zero in this language, so
;  (m + 500) / 1000 rounds correctly for positive m and wrongly for
;  negative m: -1600 would give -1 where the nearest whole degree is -2.
;  A Pi 4 below zero is unusual and not impossible - a board in an
;  unheated workshop in winter is the case that eventually turns up -
;  and a rounding rule that is only right in the common half is exactly
;  the kind of thing that is never noticed and never right.
;
;  #THERM_NONE passes through unchanged so a caller can print the same
;  sentinel test either side of the conversion.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i ThermalWholeC(milli.i)
  If milli = #THERM_NONE
    ProcedureReturn #THERM_NONE
  EndIf
  If milli >= 0
    ProcedureReturn (milli + 500) / 1000
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn -((-milli + 500) / 1000)
EndProcedure
